HR Analytics – Employee Attrition Analysis

Project Overview

This project analyzes employee attrition using HR data to identify workforce patterns and understand factors associated with employee turnover.

The goal is to help HR teams make data-driven decisions and improve employee retention.

Objectives

- Analyze overall employee attrition.
- Identify departments and job roles with high attrition.
- Study the relationship between overtime and attrition.
- Explore employee demographics and job satisfaction.
- Provide actionable HR recommendations.

Dataset

- Dataset: IBM HR Employee Attrition Dataset
- Records: 1,470 employees
- Features: 35 columns
- Target Variable: Attrition

Tools & Technologies

- Python
- Google Colab
- Pandas
- Matplotlib
- Seaborn

Project Workflow

1. Data loading and exploration
2. Data quality checks
3. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
4. Attrition analysis by department and job role
5. Demographic and work-related analysis
6. Data visualization
7. Business insights and recommendations

Key Findings

- Sales had the highest department attrition rate at 20.63%.
- Human Resources recorded 19.05% attrition.
- Research & Development recorded 13.84% attrition.
- Sales Representatives had the highest job-role attrition rate at 39.76%.

Business Recommendations

- Review sales targets, incentives and workload.
- Investigate retention challenges in high-attrition job roles.
- Monitor overtime and employee satisfaction.
- Strengthen employee engagement and career development opportunities.

Project Files

- "HR_Analytics_Employee_Attrition.ipynb" – Python analysis notebook
- "HR_Analytics_Dashboard.png" – HR analytics dashboard
- "HR_Analytics_Results.zip" – Analysis results and CSV reports

Conclusion

This project demonstrates how Python-based data analysis can help identify employee attrition patterns and support better HR decision-making.

